Destructuring assignment:

- This new feature can also catch your attention when reading ES2015 code. There is now a shortcut
    for assigning variables from objects or arrays.

    In ES5:

            var httpOptions = { timeout: 2000, isCache: true }; // object with properties: timeout, isCache
            // later
            var httpTimeout = httpOptions.timeout;  // assigning to a variable httpTimeout
            var httpCache = httpOptions.isCache;
    
    Now in ES2015, we can do:

            const httpOptions = { timeout: 2000, isCache: true };
            // later
            const { timeout: httpTimeout, isCache: httpCache } = httpOptions;
        
        -> The key is to look for property in the object and the value is the varaiable to assign.
        -> we now have variable named 'httpTimeout' and 'httpCache'
    
    Even better: if the variable we want to assign has the same name as the property, we can simply
    write:

            const httpOptions = { timeout: 2000, isCache: true };
            // later
            const { timeout, isCache } = httpOptions
        
        -> we now have variable named 'timeout' and 'isCache'

- The cool thing is that it also works with nested objects ✅:

            const httpOptions = { timeout: 2000, cache: { age: 2 } };
            // later
            const { timeout: httpTimeout, cache: { age } } = httpOptions;
        
        -> we now have variables named 'httpTimeout' and 'age'
        -> values: httpTimeout = 2000 and cache = 2

- And the same is possible with Arrays ✅:

            const timeouts = [1000, 2000, 3000];
            // later
            const [shortTimeout, mediumTimeout] = timeouts;   
            console.log(shortTimeout, mediumTimeout); 
        
        -> we now have variables 'shortTimeout' and 'mediumTimeout'
        -> values: shortTimeout = 1000 and mediumTimeout = 2000

- Of course it also works for arrays in arrays, or arrays in objects, etc ✅

- One interesting use of this can be for multiple return values. Imagine a function
    randomPonyInRace that returns a pony and its position in a race:

            function randomPonyInRace() {
                const pony = { name: 'Rainbow Dash' };
                const position = 2;
                // ...
                return { pony, position };
            }
            const { position, pony } = randomPonyInRace();

        -> The new destructuring feature assigns the position returned by the method to the position variable,
            and the pony to the pony variable! And if you don’t care about the position, you can
            write:

            function randomPonyInRace() {
                const pony = { name: 'Rainbow Dash' };
                const position = 2;
                // ...
                return { pony, position };
            }
            const { pony } = randomPonyInRace();
        
        -> and you will only have the pony!